= <span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span> Conference

# reference: Evans, Ellen L. “Adam Stegerwald and the Role of the Christian Trade Unions in the Weimar Republic.” The Catholic Historical Review 59, no. 4 (1974): 602–26.

Wilhelm Marx, former <span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span> chancellor and presidential candidate, has chosen to resign from leadership of the <span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span>. Now, the party holds a conference to replace him. Several names have been floated around, including Heinrich Brauns, Joseph Joos and Adam Stegerwald.

The party has splintered in two over the vote to decide his sucessor. The nominee of the National Committee is Christian Trade Union (<span style="color: #000000;">**DGB**</span>) chairman Adam Stegerwald, who has lain in waiting for quite some time now. On the other hand, there is Joseph Joos, whose concilliatory nature endears him to most of the party, as opposed to Stegerwald's more abrasive personality. However, if the vote is deadlocked, a dark horse could emerge as the chairman of the <span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span>. 

- @cvp_stegerwald: Adam Stegerwald has been confirmed by the rest of the party.
- @stegerwald: Adam Stegerwald has been confirmed by the rest of the party. 
- @ulitzka: The party finds an alternative in Carl Ulitzka.
- @fail: The party fails to find a suitable successsor

@cvp_stegerwald
unavailable-subtitle: [? if labor_dissent > 15: The white collar unions are still unhappy with Stegerwald's decision oppose wage increases last year. ?][? if labor_strength < 25: Stegerwald does not have enough influence to secure the nomination. ?][? if dnvp_relation < 55 : His policy of cooperation with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> is unpopular right now. ?]
subtitle: Stegerwald sits on the right of the labor faction and would rather work with the political right than the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span>.
